- How would you optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays?
- How would you adapt if exactly k windows are not required but at most k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical DP with fixed-length window constraints and reconstruction, mirroring analytics batching scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact segment count
- Prefix sums for fast interval sums
- Reconstruction path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithBounds(A, k, L, R){
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const neg = -1/0; const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(neg));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for(let len=L; len<=R && len<=i; len++){
        if (dp[i-len][t-1] !== neg) {
          const val = dp[i-len][t-1] + (P[i]-P[i-len]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = len; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], take}
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How to adapt if dominoes have variable lengths or weights?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a harder variant of partition DP where each block's value depends on its range (max-min). It requires careful state design, precomputation of subarray values, and backtracking to output exact k non-overlapping blocks.

## Key Concepts
- 2D DP over prefix index and number of blocks
- Precomputing block values V[l][r] = max - min for all subarrays
- Reconstruction via parent pointers to output endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRangeSubarrayValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const V = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[r]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      V[l][r] = mx - mn;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + V[j - 1][i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t] !== -1) {
    const j = parent[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests concrete DP skills on string partitioning with reconstruction; approachable for beginners yet non-trivial.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: maximum sum for first i digits split into t parts
- val(j,i): integer value of s[j..i-1] (leading zeros allowed)
- transition: dp[i][t] = max_{j ∈ [t-1..i-1]} dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i)
- reconstruction: track prev to recover split indices
- complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space; use BigInt for large results

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSplit(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = (a,b)=> BigInt(s.slice(a,b));
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1n));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    dp[i][1] = val(0,i);
  }

  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<i;j++){
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i);
        if(candidate > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct splits
  const splits = [];
  let curr = n, parts = k;
  while(parts > 1){
    const j = prev[curr][parts];
    splits.push(j);
    curr = j;
    parts--;
  }
  splits.reverse();

  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], splits};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow diagonal moves?
- How would you adapt for multiple starting points or 3D grids?

## Why This Is Asked
